## The problem

An endpoint that reads like this shipped in a real product last year:

```json
POST /tasks
{ "userId": "9f3c...", "title": "Renew domain" }
```

It works perfectly. The app sends the id it got at login, the server writes the row, the list refreshes. Every test passes, because every test uses the app.

Then a QA engineer copies the request out of the network tab to reproduce a bug, pastes it into a terminal, and changes one field: her teammate's user id, which she has from a shared test fixture. The task appears in his account. She tries the GET endpoint next and is reading his tasks. She reports it, which is the lucky version of this story.

The bug is not the missing check. The bug is that the endpoint asked the client who it was. A request is a text file anyone can write, your app is a bundle on a device you do not control, and `curl` does not care that you built a nice login screen.

## The idea

Identity has to arrive in a form the sender cannot forge. That form is a signed token.

A JWT is three base64 chunks separated by dots: a header saying which algorithm, a payload of claims, and a signature. The claims are not encrypted. Anyone can read them, including you, right now, with `atob(token.split('.')[1])`. What they cannot do is change one and keep the signature valid, because the signature was computed over the payload with a secret only the auth server holds.

The analogy is a festival wristband. The claims are printed on the outside where anyone can read your name and which zones you paid for. The hologram is the signature. A bouncer who **reads** the wristband has done nothing, because I can print a wristband. A bouncer who **checks the hologram** has done the entire job. In code, `decode` and `verify` look almost identical and differ by everything.

The payload carries a `sub` claim (the user id), an `email`, a `role`, and an `exp`. Your middleware's only job is to turn a verified token into `req.user`, and from then on no route ever asks the client who it is talking to.

## In practice

Scaffold the folders you will actually use. The layout mirrors phase 0: routes handle HTTP, services hold logic, config holds startup wiring.

```bash
cd ~/Documents/WorkFolder/SharedProjects/node+supabase/task-manager-api
mkdir -p src/config src/middleware src/routes src/schemas src/services
npm i express helmet cors zod pino pino-pretty dotenv @supabase/supabase-js
npm i -D typescript ts-node nodemon @types/node @types/express @types/cors
```

**`src/config/env.ts` runs before anything else and is allowed to kill the process.** Every environment variable is a string or missing, so coerce and default explicitly.

```ts
const envSchema = z.object({
  SUPABASE_URL: z.string().url(),
  SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY: z.string().min(1),
  PORT: z.coerce.number().default(3000),
  NODE_ENV: z.enum(['development', 'production', 'test']).default('development'),
});

const parsed = envSchema.safeParse(process.env);
if (!parsed.success) {
  console.error('Invalid environment:', parsed.error.flatten().fieldErrors);
  process.exit(1);
}

export const env = parsed.data;
```

Three deliberate choices there. `safeParse` rather than `parse`, so you control the exit instead of throwing a stack trace at whoever started the process. `z.coerce.number()` because `process.env.PORT` is the string `'3000'`. And `console.error` rather than the logger, because the logger reads `env` and does not exist yet, and the process is about to die anyway. This is the one place that exception is honest.

The payoff is a changed failure mode: from "random 500s three hours after deploy because a URL was empty" to "the container refused to start and the log says `SUPABASE_URL: Required`".

**`src/config/logger.ts`** gives you levels and structure. `pino` writes JSON with fields you can filter on, at `info` in production and `debug` locally, with `pino-pretty` making it readable in your terminal. It is not `console.log` because a log aggregator cannot filter a sentence, and `console.log` is synchronous and blocks the event loop under load.

**`src/middleware/auth.ts`** is the whole lesson.

```ts
export async function requireAuth(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  const header = req.headers.authorization;

  if (!header?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing or malformed Authorization header' });
    return;
  }

  const token = header.slice('Bearer '.length);
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.getUser(token);

  if (error || !data.user) {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });
    return;
  }

  req.user = { id: data.user.id, email: data.user.email ?? '' };
  next();
}
```

`getUser(token)` hands the token to Supabase's auth server, which checks the signature, checks `exp`, and checks whether the session was revoked. That third check is why this project calls out rather than verifying locally with the JWT secret. Local verification is faster, but a token from a user who logged out ten seconds ago still has a valid signature and an unexpired `exp`, so local-only verification honours it until it ages out. You are trading a round trip for the ability to log someone out. Know you are making the trade, because at high traffic you will make it the other way and add a cache.

Mount it in front of everything protected, once:

```ts
const router = Router();
router.use(requireAuth);

router.post('/tasks', async (req, res) => {
  const input = createTaskSchema.parse(req.body);
  const task = await taskService.createTask(req.user!.id, input);
  res.status(201).json(task);
});
```

`req.user!.id` is the only place a user id enters your system. `createTaskSchema` does not have a `userId` field, so a client that sends one gets it ignored rather than honoured.

## In your world

You are on both ends of this one. In your RN app the access token is short-lived and lives in memory or `expo-secure-store`, never in `AsyncStorage`, which is unencrypted on disk and readable from a rooted device or a backup. The refresh token is the long-lived credential and only ever travels to the refresh endpoint. On a 401 the client refreshes once, retries once, and signs the user out if that fails. You have consumed that flow for years; this week you own the other half.

Your work backend has the identical shape in different clothes: an auth middleware runs before handlers, resolves the caller, and puts the result somewhere the handler can read, and handlers never take identity from the body. It goes one step further by deriving the request's database handle from that identity, which is day 32.

## Watch out

- Decoding is not verifying. If a code path reads claims out of a token without checking the signature, treat that token as user input.
- `req.user!` is only safe because `router.use(requireAuth)` runs first. Mount a route above that line and the non-null assertion becomes a lie that crashes at runtime.
- 401 means "I do not know who you are", 403 means "I know who you are and you may not do this". Sending 403 for a missing token teaches your client the wrong retry behaviour.
- Never log the `Authorization` header, and never log the body of a login request. A token in a log file is a valid credential sitting in a place with looser access control than your database.
- `cors()` with no options allows every origin. That is fine on day 11 and unacceptable on the day you deploy. Leave a TODO you will actually honour.

